# AM62x silicon: GP, HS-FS, HS-SE — and which bootloader each one takes

K3 parts ship in one of three *device types*, and the boot ROM will only accept a
`tiboot3.bin` packaged for the one in front of it.

| Device type | Authenticates? | Customer keys in eFuse? | Our payloads encrypted? |
|---|---|---|---|
| **GP** — General Purpose | no | n/a | no |
| **HS-FS** — High Security, Field Securable | yes, but against *any* key | **no** | **no** |
| **HS-SE** — High Security, Security Enforced | yes, against your fused key | yes, one-way | no (unless you add it) |

U-Boot's own `doc/board/ti/k3.rst` puts HS-FS like this:

> HS-FS (High Security - Field Securable) is the state of a K3 device before it
> has been eFused with customer security keys. In the HS-FS state the
> authentication still can function as in HS-SE, but as there are no customer
> keys to verify the signatures against, the authentication will pass for
> certificates signed with any key.

**HS-FS is the "secure but no encryption and no keys" configuration**, and it is
what this BSP targets on every HS board. Concretely:

* The chain is **signed, never encrypted**. The only encrypted thing anywhere is
  TI's *own* firmware blob (`ti-fs-firmware-am62x-hs-fs-enc.bin`), which TI ships
  that way and the ROM decrypts with TI's key. Our SPL, ATF, OP-TEE, DM and
  U-Boot go in as plain signed images.
* The signing key is **U-Boot's in-tree demo key**,
  `arch/arm/mach-k3/keys/custMpk.pem`. `arch/arm/dts/k3-binman.dtsi` copies it
  into the build directory, binman signs with it, and on HS-FS the ROM accepts
  it because no customer hash is fused. The resulting certificate reads
  `CN=TI Support` and carries a 30-day validity window — harmless, because
  ROM/TIFS have no clock and do not check dates.
* **No `TI_SECURE_DEV_PKG` is needed**, and none is used here. That variable is
  for the legacy signing path; everything on these boards goes through binman.
* Nothing here fuses anything. Moving a board to HS-SE means burning your own
  MPK hash into eFuse, which is **one-way** — do not do it by accident, and note
  that the demo key above would stop working the moment you did.

## What this BSP actually flashes

| Board | Device type | `tiboot3.bin` from | `tispl.bin` / `u-boot.img` from |
|---|---|---|---|
| MYIR MYD-YM62X | GP | `tiboot3-am62x-gp-myc-am62x.bin` | `tispl.bin` / `u-boot.img` |
| TI SK-AM62B-P1 | HS-FS | `tiboot3-am62x-hs-fs-evm.bin` | `tispl.bin` / `u-boot.img` |
| BeagleBoard PocketBeagle 2 | HS-FS | `tiboot3-am62x-hs-fs-evm.bin` | `tispl.bin` / `u-boot.img` |

On **PocketBeagle 2** there is no choice to get wrong: BeagleBoard's
`arch/arm/dts/k3-am6232-pocketbeagle2-binman.dtsi` defines only two R5 images,
`tiboot3-am62x-hs-evm.bin` (HS-SE) and `tiboot3-am62x-hs-fs-evm.bin` (HS-FS),
and symlinks `tiboot3.bin` to the HS-FS one. **It builds no GP image at all.**
An earlier revision of this page said the PocketBeagle used the GP version; that
is wrong for PocketBeagle 2, and the missing GP image is the proof.

On **HS-FS and HS-SE you flash the signed `tispl.bin` and `u-boot.img`**, not the
`tispl.bin_unsigned` / `u-boot.img_unsigned` twins sitting next to them in the
same directory. `doc/board/ti/k3.rst` names the `_unsigned` pair for GP; GP
silicon does not enforce authentication either way, and this BSP's MYIR chain
uses the signed pair in practice.

Watch the **`tiboot3.bin` symlink**: binman points it at whichever variant the
board file declares, which is not always the one you want. On the MYIR board
`k3-myc-am62x-dev-binman.dtsi` symlinks it to the **hs-fs** image even though the
silicon is GP, so `build-spare-sd.sh` names the gp file explicitly. On the SK and
PocketBeagle 2 the symlink happens to be right — but name the file anyway.

Flashing the wrong variant fails **silently**: the ROM rejects the image before
the UART comes up, so the board looks dead rather than printing an error.

## Two silent failure modes, and how to catch both

The second one is easier to hit than the first. U-Boot invokes binman with
`--allow-missing --fake-ext-blobs` (see `cmd_binman` in its `Makefile`), so a
build with no `BINMAN_INDIRS` **succeeds** — it just writes zero-byte stand-ins
for the TI firmware into `<out>/binman-fake/` and packages those. The result is
the right size, has a real certificate, and contains no TIFS at all. The TIFS
*stub* inside `tispl.bin` is worse: `k3-*-binman.dtsi` marks it `optional;`, so
binman does not even warn.

`res/uboot/check-bootloader.sh` checks the artifacts rather than the build log —
it searches the images for the actual firmware bytes:

```sh
./res/uboot/check-bootloader.sh u-boot-pb/out_bp2                    # PocketBeagle 2, HS-FS
./res/uboot/check-bootloader.sh u-boot-official/out_sk               # SK, HS-FS
VARIANT=gp ./res/uboot/check-bootloader.sh u-boot-official/out_myir  # MYIR, GP
```

It verifies the variant, the `tiboot3.bin` symlink, that the variant's real TIFS
blob is embedded, that the ROM certificate parses, that the signed `tispl.bin` /
`u-boot.img` exist and differ from their unsigned twins, and that the TIFS stub
made it into `tispl.bin` — then prints the exact `build-spare-sd.sh` variables.
Non-zero exit means do not flash.

A populated `binman-fake/` is reported as a warning, not a failure: binman
creates that directory on every build and never cleans it, so leftovers from an
earlier bad run are common. The embedded-blob checks are the authoritative ones.

## Confirming the silicon itself

Two ways, both on the board rather than on the host:

```
U-Boot SPL 2025.04 ...
SoC:   AM62X SR1.0 HS-FS          <- or GP, or HS-SE
```

or, if it will not boot far enough to print that, put it in UART boot, capture
the SoC ID dump the ROM emits and decode it:

```sh
./res/parse_uart_boot_socid.py socid.txt
# DeviceType           : HS-FS      (or GP)
```

The `DeviceType` field is the ROM's own answer and is the one to trust.

## References

* TI, [Foundational Components Migration Guide — device types](https://software-dl.ti.com/processor-sdk-linux/esd/AM62X/10_00_07_04/exports/docs/linux/Foundational_Components_Migration_Guide.html)
* U-Boot, `doc/board/ti/k3.rst` — "Secure Boot" and the chain of trust
* U-Boot, `arch/arm/dts/k3-binman.dtsi` — where `custMpk.pem` comes from
* BeagleBoard U-Boot fork, `arch/arm/dts/k3-am6232-pocketbeagle2-binman.dtsi` —
  the PocketBeagle 2 image list (`u-boot-pb/`, cloned by `fetch.sh`)

Carried over from the original note on this page: the MYIR board appears to use
an older silicon revision than the other two, which is consistent with it being
the only GP part here.  That note also called the PocketBeagle a GP board — see
above for why that does not hold for PocketBeagle 2.
